    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Between 1580 and the 1820s well over a million people from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> were shipped as slaves to the New World, mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and Oliver and Atmore called the colony 'a gigantic slave-trading enterprise' for 200 years.</li><li>Portugal ruled only the coast in the 16th and 17th centuries; full control of the territory came at the beginning of the 20th century, through wars of conquest and agreements with other European powers.</li><li>Forced labour lasted until 1962, and the war for independence that began in 1961 ended only after the Carnation Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, with independence on 11 November 1975.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1640 to 1648 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, ruled as Fort Aardenburgh: the Dutch, who had seized north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, wanted slaves for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> plantations.</p></aside>
    </section>

