    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Portuguese presence began with the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a>, who reached the Kongo Kingdom in 1484. The land was then home to a number of separate peoples, some organised as kingdoms or federations. The Portuguese, interested above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, kept a peaceful and profitable relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>'s rulers and nobles; kings such as João I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a> studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, learned Portuguese and shared the profits of the trade. The Portuguese set up small trading posts on the lower Congo and a more important settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>, and from the start their expeditions aimed at the conversion of the peoples they met, as they had since the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415.</p><p>In 1575 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">São Paulo de Loanda</a>, Luanda, on the coast south of the Kongo Kingdom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benguela">Benguela</a> followed in the 17th century, and the town of Mbaka was founded in 1618. For two centuries Portugal held the coast and fought the kingdoms inland. A mestiço elite emerged in Luanda by the early 17th century, its wealth drawn from buying captives from the interior, and the Afro-Portuguese communities called 'Ambaquista' grew where the colony reached inland. Portugal annexed what remained of the Kingdom of Ndongo after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pungo-andongo">Pungo Andongo</a>, and in the 18th century gained control of most of the central highlands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between 1580 and the 1820s well over a million people were shipped from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> as slaves, mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Most were captured by other Africans in wars and raids, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbundu">Mbundu</a> slave hunters, and sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>'s merchants; Brazilian ships were the most numerous in Luanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benguela">Benguela</a>, so that Angola was in effect a colony of Brazil. From 1764 the economy slowly shifted to production for home use and export; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> was abolished in 1836, ruining the mestiço elite, and by 1850 Luanda exported palm and peanut oil, wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa.</p><p>From 1908 a 'native tax' pushed Africans into the money economy. Forced labour took many forms, the harshest shipping workers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome-and-principe">São Tomé and Príncipe</a>, from which few returned; in 1954 over 300,000 Angolans were estimated to work under forced labour, which was abolished only in 1962. Edward Ross, reporting to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1924, called the system 'virtually state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>'. Diamonds were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> in 1912, and Diamang, granted the concession in 1917, became the largest commercial operator, its wealth made by African labourers, many forcibly recruited. Iron ore was mined from the mid-1950s, petroleum was found in 1955, and in 1973 oil overtook coffee as the largest export.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> was governed in a highly militaristic style. The Portuguese Army generally kept about 2,000 European soldiers there from the 16th century into the 20th, backed by the guerra preta ('black war') of 5,000 to 20,000 African troops raised by loyal sobas. Service was unpopular: most soldiers were degredados, convicts sent to ease prison overcrowding, deserters and adventurers, and only in the final campaigns of conquest of 1890 to 1920 were first-rate officers posted there regularly. Portugal defeated the Kongo Kingdom at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a> on 29 October 1665, but was disastrously defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kitombo">Battle of Kitombo</a> in 1670.</p><p>The Campaigns of Pacification and Occupation began in 1838, and Viye was annexed in 1890. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> German forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> took Naulila in December 1914, and several peoples revolted, among them the German-backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Ovambo</a> Uprising of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandume-ya-ndemufayo">Mandume ya Ndemufayo</a> of Oukwanyama. In March 1915 about 10,000 troops under General António Júlio da Costa Pereira de Eça reoccupied the south, massacring the population, burning villages and deepening a famine; a 2026 study described the campaign as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Between 1939 and 1943 army operations against the Mucubal killed hundreds and sent 3,529 prisoners to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Every Captain-General, renamed Governor-General in 1836, was a serving army or naval officer. E. Silva Correia wrote in 1782 that in no part of the Portuguese world was an army more necessary than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and Governor Calheiros e Menezes in 1867 that 'the normal condition of the administration of this colony is to make war'. In the 20th century Angola was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> regime. In 1951 it became an overseas province, legally as much a part of Portugal as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>; government was highly centralised, with authority running from the Prime Minister's office in Lisbon down a rigid chain of command, and nearly all officials came from Portugal.</p><p>The governor-general, appointed by the Portuguese cabinet on the Overseas Minister's recommendation, held both executive and legislative authority, advised by a Government Council and served by five secretaries; a Legislative Council of limited powers approved the budget, and an Economic and Social Council had to be consulted on all draft legislation. From 1967 Angola sent delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. The Organic Law of June 1972 made Angola an autonomous state, the 'State of Angola' from 1 January 1973, with Portugal keeping defence and foreign relations, and elections for a legislative assembly were held in 1973; but the changes were extremely limited and the new government was the old provincial one under other titles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For two centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> was bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and its middle passage, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions carried Brazilian influence in religion and education. Portugal subjugated the neighbouring states of the highlands, among them Bié and Bailundo, one by one. The borders of present-day Angola were drawn by agreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1885–1886, the Anglo-Portuguese Treaty of 1891 and the arbitration of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> in 1905; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-simulambuco">Treaty of Simulambuco</a> of 1885 made Cabinda a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, later joined to the colony. The territory covered 1,246,700 square kilometres.</p><p>After 1951 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> encouraged mass settlement: settlers numbered 80,000 in 1950 and 335,000 by 1974, the second largest white population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and they owned almost all businesses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations. Society in the 1950s was described as one of 'extreme racism', with whites at the top, mestiços below them, then assimilados, and the unassimilated African majority at the bottom; settlers' children had schools while Africans had almost none. Education for Africans widened from the 1950s, and the Estudos Gerais Universitários de Angola, founded in 1962, became the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in 1968.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The settlers' boom, and the regime's open favour to them, angered the African population. In the late 1950s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> organised against Portuguese rule and forced labour. On 15 March 1961 Holden Roberto led 4,000 to 5,000 militants into the north, killing at least 1,000 whites and an unknown number of blacks; Portugal abolished the Estatuto do Indigenato that September and sent thousands of troops. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> were divided by ethnicity, ideology and leadership: the MPLA drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbundu">Mbundu</a>, mestiços and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, the FNLA on the Bakongo, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>, founded by Jonas Savimbi in 1966, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a>. The MPLA's push into the east from 1966 was driven back by the Portuguese campaign of 1971, but the movements were never fully defeated.</p><p>The long colonial war, international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> and its cost at home led to the mutiny of Portuguese officers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 25 April 1974, which overthrew the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-caetano">Marcelo Caetano</a>; in May the new junta proclaimed a truce with the guerrillas. Hundreds of thousands of Portuguese citizens, the retornados, left as refugees. On 11 November 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> became a sovereign state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a>, as the People's Republic of Angola, which re-annexed the short-lived Republic of Cabinda.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> began as the coastal settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> in 1575 and for two centuries was chiefly a slave-trading colony tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Conquered inland only by the early 20th century and worked by forced labour until 1962, it was settled heavily after 1951, fought a war for independence from 1961 and became independent in 1975.</em></p>

